\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\title{Recovery of a Kelp Forest After the 2024 Marine Heatwave}
\author{R. Calloway and P. Ndlovu}
\date{June 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
So basically, the huge marine heatwave in the summer of 2024 totally
wrecked the kelp forest we've been monitoring since 2019. We kept going
back every month for two full years and counted kelp plants and fish
along eight fixed transects. Right after the heatwave the kelp pretty
much collapsed, which was awful to watch. But here's the cool part:
after about 18 months the kelp started bouncing back way faster than
anyone had expected, and by month 24 the canopy cover was back to
roughly 85\% of what it was before the heatwave. The fish took their
time, though --- their numbers recovered much more slowly. We reckon
the fast comeback happened because the heatwave didn't wipe out the
understory turf like everyone assumed, so the young kelp still had
something to grow back from.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Marine heatwaves are increasing in frequency and duration, and their
consequences for foundation species remain poorly resolved. Kelp
forests are among the most productive coastal ecosystems, yet several
recent events have shown how quickly a canopy can be lost when
temperature anomalies persist for weeks.

\section{Monitoring Design}
Eight permanent transects were established in 2019 across two sites on
the northern headland. Divers recorded kelp stipe counts, canopy cover
and fish abundance on each transect at monthly intervals, weather
permitting, from January 2023 to December 2025.

\section{Outlook}
The monitoring programme continues, and a full analysis of the fish
community data will follow in a companion paper.
\end{document}
